<Layout title="Ketamine Over Time: Evidence on Long-Term Use and Its Implications for Informed Choice">
    <header>
      <h1>Ketamine Over Time: Evidence on Long-Term Use and Its Implications for Informed Choice</h1>
      <div class="metadata">
        <time datetime="2026-09-28">Sep 28, 2026</time>
        <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
        <span class="author">Shasta Fox</span>
      </div>
    </header><blockquote><strong>Research essay.</strong> Author-submitted narrative interpretation. Full reference verification and independent peer review remain pending. Evidence from frequent nonmedical ketamine use is distinct from evidence on supervised treatment; associations do not establish causation.</blockquote>

    <div>
      <section>
        <p class="lead-paragraph">People who use ketamine often do so for reasons that deserve to be taken seriously. The drug acts within minutes, produces a sense of distance from distress, and in clinical trials has reduced suicidal thoughts within a day of administration (Pl&ouml;derl et al., 2025). These effects help explain its growing use both inside and outside medicine. A separate body of research, however, examines what happens when ketamine use becomes frequent and sustained. That research does not show that every use leads to harm. It shows that risks accumulate with frequency, dose, and combination with other substances, and that some of those harms persist after use stops. This paper summarizes that evidence so that people who use ketamine, and those who support them, can make decisions based on accurate information rather than on either reassurance or alarm.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Mortality and Polysubstance Use</h2>
        <p>National surveillance data from England, Wales, and Northern Ireland provide one recent picture of ketamine-related deaths. Pullen et al. (2026) identified 696 deaths between 1999 and 2024 in which illicit ketamine was detected at post-mortem, with annual deaths rising from 15 in 2014 to a projected 197 in 2024. The same analysis clarifies where risk is concentrated. The proportion of deaths in which ketamine itself was implicated fell from 60.0% to 42.6% over that decade, while the median number of co-administered substances rose from three in the early 2000s to six in 2020&ndash;2024. Decedents were also increasingly drawn from deprived areas and contexts of dependence. The authors concluded that effective responses must extend beyond drug classification to harm reduction, treatment, and social support (Pullen et al., 2026). Detection after death does not establish that ketamine caused the death, and these data do not provide a risk per user or prove the independent effect of social isolation. Polysubstance exposure is an important feature of this series.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Cognitive, Affective, and Urological Effects of Frequent Use</h2>

        <h3>Memory, Language, and Brain Structure</h3>
        <p>The clearest longitudinal evidence comes from a one-year study of 150 participants. Frequent ketamine users showed impairments in spatial working memory and pattern recognition memory, and the degree of impairment increased with the amount used (Morgan et al., 2010). A cross-sectional study found that chronic users also had deficits in verbal fluency and verbal learning, and that verbal learning declined in proportion to estimated lifetime use (Chan et al., 2013). Neuroimaging offers a structural correlate: among 41 ketamine-dependent individuals, gray matter volume in the frontal cortex was reduced relative to 44 controls, and the reduction was associated with duration of use and cumulative dose (Liao et al., 2011). Because these studies are observational, they cannot fully exclude differences that preceded ketamine use; the dose-dependent pattern, however, is consistent with a causal contribution.</p>

        <h3>Mood</h3>
        <p>The same longitudinal study found that depressive symptoms among frequent users increased over the year of observation, as did delusional symptoms (Morgan et al., 2010). This finding does not contradict ketamine's short-term antidepressant effect, but it suggests that frequent, unsupervised use may be associated with worsening mood over longer periods.</p>

        <h3>Urological Effects</h3>
        <p>Heavy use is also associated with ketamine-induced cystitis, an inflammatory condition of the bladder (Anderson et al., 2022). Because this harm affects an organ system unrelated to the drug's intended effects, it may go unrecognized until symptoms develop.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Ketamine as a Treatment for Depression</h2>
        <p>Many people turn to ketamine, whether prescribed or not, to relieve depression, and the short-term evidence supports that motivation. A 2025 systematic review of 66 trials with 5,103 participants found that ketamine reduced suicidal ideation for up to approximately two weeks (Pl&ouml;derl et al., 2025). The same review, however, found that the effect diminished thereafter, that repeated doses were not superior to single doses, that esketamine's effect was negligible after 24 hours, and that the evidence was insufficient to show a reduction in suicidal behavior. The authors also noted that unblinding bias may have inflated short-term estimates; the review is a preprint and had not completed peer review at the time of writing. An earlier systematic review found that only a minority of studies of ketamine for depression assessed long-term safety (Short et al., 2018). A review of maintenance treatment in treatment-resistant depression reported that serious harms appeared uncommon under clinical supervision, although it rested on only three randomized trials (Smith-Apeldoorn et al., 2022). Taken together, these findings indicate genuine short-term benefit and an unresolved question about the balance of benefit and harm with sustained use.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Longer-Term Supervised Treatment</h2><p>Later clinical evidence adds an important distinction. The open-label SUSTAIN-3 interim study followed 1,148 participants receiving supervised esketamine plus an oral antidepressant, with exposure up to 4.5 years. Depression improvement generally persisted among those remaining in treatment, and no new safety signal was identified (Zaki et al., 2023). Selection of continuing patients, attrition, and the absence of a randomized long-term comparator limit causal conclusions. These results neither establish risk-free treatment nor justify transferring findings from heavy nonmedical ketamine use directly to supervised esketamine.</p></section><section><h2>Indicators for Self-Assessment</h2>
        <p>The research above suggests several observable indicators that a person may use to evaluate their own pattern of use. These include needing larger amounts to achieve the same effect; using more often than intended; combining ketamine with other substances more frequently; noticing lapses in memory or difficulty finding words; changes in relationships, work, or responsibilities that others have remarked upon; and increased urinary frequency, urgency, or discomfort. These indicators are not diagnostic. They are offered as information that a person can weigh in light of their own goals and circumstances.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Pathways to Change</h2>
        <p>Because harms concentrate in frequent use, high doses, and polysubstance use (Morgan et al., 2010; Pullen et al., 2026), meaningful change need not be all-or-nothing. Avoiding combinations with other substances, reducing frequency, and discontinuing use each address a distinct source of risk. People who wish to discuss their options can contact the US <a href="https://www.samhsa.gov/find-help/helplines/national-helpline">SAMHSA National Helpline</a> (<a href="tel:18006624357">1-800-662-4357</a>), which is free and available 24 hours a day. People receiving prescribed treatment should discuss changes with their treating clinician. A primary care clinician can also evaluate bladder and kidney function for those who have used ketamine heavily.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>The evidence does not support describing ketamine as either harmless or uniformly dangerous. It supports a more specific conclusion: ketamine's risks increase with frequency, dose, and combination with other substances, and some of its harms to memory, language, mood, and the bladder may persist beyond the period of use. Benefits after an isolated dose may be short-lived; maintenance treatment is a separate question, with evidence of sustained benefit in some supervised patients. Suicidal-ideation outcomes must not be treated as interchangeable with overall depression outcomes or suicide prevention. People who use ketamine are best served by accurate information about these patterns, presented in a way that respects their capacity to decide.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Rationale for an Autonomy-Supportive Approach</h2>
        <p>This paper deliberately avoids fear appeals and directive language. Messages perceived as threatening personal freedom can provoke psychological reactance, a motivational state in which people act to restore the threatened freedom, sometimes by doing the opposite of what a message advises (Brehm, 1966). Motivational interviewing offers an alternative: it supports autonomy, provides information without pressure, and invites people to examine their own reasons for change (Miller &amp; Rollnick, 2013). The persuasive strategy here therefore rests on accuracy and respect rather than on exaggeration; empirical claims should be evaluated against the cited research and its limitations.</p>
      </section>

